      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{x+1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
